%I A399724 #29 Nov 26 2026 21:12:37
%S A399724 4,8,12,16,20,24,28,208,820,1048,1588,1828,2248,2308,2548,2728,6508,
%T A399724 8428
%N A399724 Multiples of 4 that are not the sum of four (not necessarily distinct) twin prime pairs.
%C A399724 A twin prime pair (p, p+2) contributes p + (p+2) = 2*m, where m is a term of A014574. So N is the sum of four twin prime pairs iff N/2 is a sum of four (not necessarily distinct) terms of A014574; so a sum of four twin prime pairs is divisible by 4 and at least 32.
%C A399724 No further terms below 10^9. It is conjectured that the sequence is finite.
%C A399724 A proof that the sequence is finite would imply the twin prime conjecture, since if there were only finitely many twin primes, only finitely many numbers would be sums of four twin prime pairs.
%C A399724 Apparently, all multiples of 4 that are at least 8*k are the sum of k twin prime pairs for k = 5, 6, 7, with no exceptions up to 2*10^7.
%e A399724 4, 8, ..., 28 are terms since the smallest sum of four twin prime pairs is 4*8 = 32.
%e A399724 208 is a term: the sums p + (p+2) of twin prime pairs not exceeding 208 are 8, 12, 24, 36, 60, 84, 120, 144, 204, and no four of them (repetitions allowed) add up to 208.
%e A399724 204 = 24 + 36 + 60 + 84 and 212 = 8 + 24 + 60 + 120 are not terms.
%o A399724 (Python)
%o A399724 from sympy import isprime
%o A399724 def is_sum4(n):
%o A399724     T = [m for m in range(4, n+1, 2) if isprime(m-1) and isprime(m+1)]
%o A399724     S = set(T)
%o A399724     for i, a in enumerate(T):
%o A399724         for j in range(i, len(T)):
%o A399724             b = T[j]
%o A399724             if a + b > n: break
%o A399724             r = n - a - b
%o A399724             for c in T[j:]:
%o A399724                 if 2*c > r: break
%o A399724                 if r - c in S: return True
%o A399724     return False
%o A399724 print([N for N in range(4, 9000, 4) if not is_sum4(N//2)])
%Y A399724 Cf. A014574, A086169, A399960.
